1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,165
Linggiu Reservoir: The Dam Singapore Built

2
00:00:03,245 --> 00:00:06,800
Inside Another Country
to Secure Its Own Water

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:11,318
In 1962 Singapore signed
a treaty guaranteeing it

4
00:00:11,398 --> 00:00:17,136
up to 250 million gallons of
water a day from the Johor River,

5
00:00:17,216 --> 00:00:20,500
a legal right that runs until 2061.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,475
That guarantee turned out
not to be enough on its own.

7
00:00:24,475 --> 00:00:28,624
In November 1990 Singapore
agreed to pay the entire cost of

8
00:00:28,704 --> 00:00:33,068
building a dam and reservoir
inside Johor — land it didn't own

9
00:00:33,148 --> 00:00:37,369
and had no authority over — for
no reason other than to make

10
00:00:37,449 --> 00:00:41,750
sure the treaty it already held
would actually keep working.

11
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:45,519
This blog told part of this
story already, in a post

12
00:00:45,599 --> 00:00:49,295
about Marina Barrage — the
dam Singapore eventually

13
00:00:49,375 --> 00:00:53,150
built at home to end its
water dependency for good.

14
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:58,222
Linggiu Reservoir is an earlier,
quieter chapter of that same anxiety,

15
00:00:58,302 --> 00:01:02,350
and the fix this time wasn't
built in Singapore at all.

16
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:06,636
The 1962 Johor River Agreement,
signed alongside the 1961

17
00:01:06,716 --> 00:01:10,772
Tebrau and Scudai Rivers
Agreement, gave Singapore the

18
00:01:10,852 --> 00:01:15,214
legal right to draw up to 250
million gallons of raw water

19
00:01:15,294 --> 00:01:19,503
a day from the Johor River,
at three Malaysian cents per

20
00:01:19,583 --> 00:01:24,025
thousand gallons — a price
fixed for the life of the deal.

21
00:01:24,025 --> 00:01:26,755
But a treaty right is not the same thing

22
00:01:26,835 --> 00:01:29,575
as water that's actually there to draw.

23
00:01:29,575 --> 00:01:32,769
Singapore's extraction
point sits at Kota Tinggi,

24
00:01:32,849 --> 00:01:37,646
close enough to the sea that in a dry
spell, when the river's flow drops,

25
00:01:37,726 --> 00:01:40,920
seawater pushes back
upstream past the intake and

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,875
the water arriving at the pumps
turns too saline to treat.

27
00:01:44,875 --> 00:01:47,918
No agreement, however watertight on paper,

28
00:01:47,998 --> 00:01:50,675
could stop a river from running low.

29
00:01:50,675 --> 00:01:54,800
Singapore's answer was to
control the river itself.

30
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:58,715
From 1989, its Public Utilities
Board began work on a dam

31
00:01:58,795 --> 00:02:02,570
across Sungai Linggiu, a
tributary that feeds the Johor

32
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:05,514
River upstream of the Kota Tinggi intake —

33
00:02:05,594 --> 00:02:10,770
holding back wet-season flow and
releasing it during dry spells to keep the

34
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:14,775
river fresh enough, that far
downstream, to draw at all.

35
00:02:14,775 --> 00:02:19,393
An environmental impact assessment,
contested for a time between the

36
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:23,125
two governments over who had
actually commissioned it,

37
00:02:23,205 --> 00:02:28,525
concluded in September 1990 that the
project's benefits outweighed its costs.

38
00:02:28,525 --> 00:02:31,820
The supplementary Water
Agreement covering it

39
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:34,820
was signed on the 24th of November 1990,

40
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:39,025
between Singapore's PUB and
the Johor state government.

41
00:02:39,025 --> 00:02:42,769
Johor's Mentri Besar at the
time, Muhyiddin Yassin —

42
00:02:42,849 --> 00:02:47,990
later Malaysia's prime minister —
said publicly that the new deal would

43
00:02:48,070 --> 00:02:52,850
ease some of the friction the
original 1962 agreement had caused.

44
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:57,347
Singapore bore the full cost,
construction and maintenance both,

45
00:02:57,427 --> 00:03:01,575
of infrastructure sitting
entirely inside another country.

46
00:03:01,575 --> 00:03:05,939
Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong made
a short working visit to Johor

47
00:03:06,019 --> 00:03:09,967
in February 1991 specifically
to see the work in progress,

48
00:03:10,047 --> 00:03:15,106
and Johor's Department of Environment
assured the public that the valley's

49
00:03:15,186 --> 00:03:19,700
flora and fauna would not be
destroyed when the site was flooded.

50
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:23,406
The dam, its reservoir
and the surrounding saddle

51
00:03:23,486 --> 00:03:28,043
embankments were completed in
April 1993, at a total cost of

52
00:03:28,123 --> 00:03:32,602
310 million Singapore dollars
— paid entirely by Singapore,

53
00:03:32,682 --> 00:03:36,700
for a facility it did not,
in the end, own outright.

54
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:41,217
The same 1990 agreement also
let Singapore draw beyond the

55
00:03:41,297 --> 00:03:46,131
daily 250-million-gallon cap
when it needed to, with the extra

56
00:03:46,211 --> 00:03:50,650
water treated in Johor and
priced by a separate formula.

57
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:53,526
But the dam's real purpose was defensive:

58
00:03:53,606 --> 00:03:58,211
keep enough water moving down the
Johor River, even in a drought,

59
00:03:58,291 --> 00:04:02,400
to protect an entitlement
Singapore already had on paper.

60
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,490
The dam did its job
quietly for two decades,

61
00:04:05,570 --> 00:04:09,100
until a regional drought
made its limits visible.

62
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,954
Through 2015 Linggiu Reservoir's
water level fell steadily —

63
00:04:14,034 --> 00:04:19,050
from just over half full in
August to 41 per cent by October.

64
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:23,097
In the middle of that same drought,
Singapore's response was to

65
00:04:23,177 --> 00:04:26,765
agree to supply Johor with
more treated water, not less;

66
00:04:26,845 --> 00:04:31,678
Johor's own taps were running low too,
and the two countries' water systems

67
00:04:31,758 --> 00:04:35,950
turned out to be tied together
in both directions, not just one.

68
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:40,005
By April 2016 Linggiu had
fallen to a new record low of

69
00:04:40,085 --> 00:04:44,214
36.9 per cent, and for the
first time in years Singapore

70
00:04:44,294 --> 00:04:48,950
officials found themselves talking
publicly about just how much

71
00:04:49,030 --> 00:04:53,386
of the country's daily supply
still depended on a reservoir

72
00:04:53,466 --> 00:04:57,450
whose rainfall it had no
control over, dam or no dam.

73
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:00,953
Singapore's real answer
to that vulnerability,

74
00:05:01,033 --> 00:05:03,525
in the end, wasn't a bigger dam.

75
00:05:03,525 --> 00:05:08,159
Weeks into the same 2015 drought,
PUB announced a fourth desalination

76
00:05:08,239 --> 00:05:11,916
plant for Marina East — one
piece of the "Four National

77
00:05:11,996 --> 00:05:15,809
Taps" strategy (local catchment,
imported water, NEWater,

78
00:05:15,889 --> 00:05:20,660
and desalination) that has spent two
decades quietly shrinking how much

79
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:24,975
of Singapore's water still needs
to cross the Causeway at all.

80
00:05:24,975 --> 00:05:29,889
Linggiu Reservoir still supplies
water to Singapore today,

81
00:05:29,969 --> 00:05:35,050
and the 1962 agreement behind
it doesn't expire until 2061.

82
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:39,174
But the reservoir Singapore
once paid 310 million Singapore

83
00:05:39,254 --> 00:05:43,307
dollars to build, on land it
didn't own, purely to protect

84
00:05:43,387 --> 00:05:47,012
a promise it was already
owed, is no longer the last

85
00:05:47,092 --> 00:05:51,225
line of defence it was built
to be — just one tap of four.

86
00:05:51,225 --> 00:05:55,279
Why it matters today: most
people who know anything about

87
00:05:55,359 --> 00:05:59,341
Singapore's water story know
Marina Barrage or NEWater —

88
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:03,700
visible, homegrown, built
entirely on Singapore's own soil.

89
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:06,775
Almost nobody remembers
that decades earlier,

90
00:06:06,855 --> 00:06:10,983
the country's answer to the
same anxiety was to reach across

91
00:06:11,063 --> 00:06:14,840
a border and build a dam
inside someone else's country,

92
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,075
just to keep a treaty
promise it already had.

93
00:06:18,075 --> 00:06:20,798
Malaysia still supplies roughly half

94
00:06:20,878 --> 00:06:23,525
of Singapore's water demand today.

95
00:06:23,525 --> 00:06:28,223
PUB's own target is to close
that gap entirely by around 2060,

96
00:06:28,303 --> 00:06:32,846
when NEWater and desalination
together are projected to meet

97
00:06:32,926 --> 00:06:38,086
some 80 per cent of demand, with
local catchment covering the rest —

98
00:06:38,166 --> 00:06:42,250
a year before the 1962
agreement itself even expires.

99
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:46,304
Linggiu Reservoir was never
meant to be the permanent answer,

100
00:06:46,384 --> 00:06:50,031
only a very expensive way of
buying time until the rest

101
00:06:50,111 --> 00:06:53,893
of the country's own taps
could catch up; on the timeline

102
00:06:53,973 --> 00:06:57,700
Singapore has set for itself,
that time is almost gone.
